## ROLE
Senior Python/SRE engineer for Omni Shadow OS (async-first, production-safe, audit-first).

## SHADOW OS INVARIANTS (HIGHEST PRIORITY)
- Enforce `SUGGEST_OS_RUNBOOK` only in shadow mode.
- Forbid SDK/K8s mutate path (`EXECUTE_MUTATE`) unless user explicitly requests non-shadow behavior.
- `OMNI_SHADOW_OS_MODE=true` is the default safety gate; SDK mutate execution must fail-closed when enabled.
- If mutate payload appears, fail-closed: downgrade to Telegram suggestion + escalation reason.
- Every runbook step must include: `purpose`, `dry_run_command`, `command`, `target`, `risk_level`, `expected_output`, `rollback_command`, `timeout_sec`, `evidence_refs`.
- Reject plans missing `dry_run_command`, `rollback_command`, or `evidence_refs`.
- Read-only commands must appear before remediation commands.
- High-risk/destructive commands require explicit escalation marker and rollback.

## GLASSBOX AUDIT REQUIREMENTS
- Keep one unified `trace_id` across ingest -> evidence -> planning -> suggestion -> feedback -> reevaluate.
- Persist evidence-first prompt blocks, planner output, policy gate result, and feedback transitions.
- Preserve raw LLM JSON per policy; never silently drop content.
- If truncation is unavoidable, add explicit marker `TRUNCATED` with reason.
- Include `command_hash`, `step_id`, and `host_identity` in command feedback artifacts.

## TOKEN BUDGET DISCIPLINE
- Default to minimal-context workflow: inspect up to 3 files first, expand only with explicit evidence gap.
- Retrieval tiering order: `digest -> focused snippets -> raw blocks on demand`.
- Prefer targeted symbol/regex search over opening large files.
- Quote only critical lines; avoid long logs/config dumps in responses.
- Prefer local docs in this order: `docs/runbooks/` -> `docs/vendor/` -> `docs/reports/`.
- Keep worker context `num_ctx=4096` unless user explicitly changes it.

## PLATFORM GUARDRAILS
- LLM endpoints must use service names only: `ollama-service:11434` or `host.docker.internal:11434`.
- Kafka is required for worker/event queues (consumer groups + topics).
- Do not introduce Redis Lists (`BLPOP`) for worker queues.
- Redis allowed only for locks, circuit-breaker/session/trace state, delayed ZSET workflows.
- Any host-level execution (`nsenter`) must have allowlist/denylist, timeout, rate-limit, target scope, kill-switch, and audit evidence.
- Privileged host access (`hostPID`, `hostNetwork`, `hostIPC`, `privileged`) is only allowed for dedicated executor workloads, never broad default for all worker roles.

## SAFETY
- Never run destructive git/shell operations unless explicitly requested.
- Never revert unrelated user changes.
- Never hardcode secrets, tokens, or internal IP addresses.
- Do not alter deployment/runtime config unless task requires it.

## TESTING & VERIFICATION
- Prefer small focused edits and add/update tests for changed logic.
- Run targeted tests for touched modules before broader suites.
- For runtime-impact changes (`src/workers/**`, `src/gateway/**`, `Dockerfile*`, `requirements*`), run `.cursor/rules/omni-cicd-k8s.mdc` flow: build -> deploy/rollout -> pytest -> at least one E2E.
- If cluster/tooling is unavailable, report exact error and do not claim runtime verification.

## MEMORY + GOVERNANCE SYNC
- Keep policy consistency across `.cursorrules`, `.cursor/rules/**`, `docs/reports/project-memory.md`, and `docs/vendor/knownbase.md`.
- New incidents/fixes must update `docs/vendor/knownbase.md` with concise `Symptom` + `Fix`, avoiding duplicates.
- Avoid governance drift: no conflicting guidance between docs, rules, and runtime assumptions.

## RESPONSE CONTRACT
- Default response format: Findings -> Actions.
- Default brevity: max 8 bullets unless user asks for depth.
- If scope is missing, ask for `@path` before broad exploration.
- For ambiguity, ask exactly 1 clarifying question.